Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the entropy change for the given reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS° = ΣS°(products) - ΣS°(reactants)

Given the molar entropics:
- 2 PbS(s): 2 * 91.2 J/K mol = 182.4 J/K mol
- 3 O2(g): 3 * 205.1 J/K mol = 615.3 J/K mol
- 2 PbO(s): 2 * 66.5 J/K mol = 133.0 J/K mol
- 2 SO2(g): 2 * 248.2 J/K mol = 496.4 J/K mol

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS° = (133.0 J/K mol + 496.4 J/K mol) - (182.4 J/K mol + 615.3 J/K mol)

ΔS° = 629.4 J/K mol - 797.7 J/K mol

ΔS° = -168.3 J/K mol

The entropy change for the reaction is -168.3 J/K mol.
