","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is mixed with a solution containing \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ti}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution, the insoluble sulfide precipitates form. The solubility of metal sulphides decreases with the ionization potential of the metal. The correct answer is:

1. **Copper(II) sulphide (\( \text{CuS} \)):** \( \text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{CuS} \) (Ksp is very low, precipitates first).
2. **Hydrogen sulfide gas is added to the solution, and the pH is maintained at 10-11 (acided aqueous solution):**
   - \( \text{Mn}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{MnS} \) (Ksp is low, precipitates)
   - \( \text{Hg}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{HgS} \) (Ksp is low, precipitates)
   - \( \text{Ni}^{2+} + \text{S}^{2-} \rightarrow \text{NiS} \) (Ksp is relatively high, may not precipitate at low \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) concentration).

Thus, the precipitates formed are \(\text{CuS}\), \(\text{MnS}\), and \(\text{HgS}\).

