\[
\Delta E \Delta t \approx \hbar
\]
where \( \Delta E \) is the uncertainty in the energy (\( \Delta E = \Delta m_i c^2 \)) and \( \Delta t \) is the mean nuclear scattering time (\( \Delta t \)) (which is the particle's decay time). The Planck constant is \( \hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s} = 5.3 \times 10^{-41} \text{ MeV s} \).

Given \( \Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s} \), we can find \( \Delta E \):
\[
\Delta E = \frac{\hbar}{\Delta t} = \frac{5.3 \times 10^{-41} \text{ MeV s}}{2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 2.65 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the uncertainty in the measured mass is:
\[
\Delta m_i = \frac{\Delta E}{c^2} = \frac{2.65 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}}{(3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})^2}
\]
However, we are working in MeV, so it is easier to use \( c^2 = 9 \times 10^{16} \text{ MeV}^2/\text{m}^2 \). Thus:
\[
\Delta m_i = \frac{2.65 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}}{9 \times 10^{16} \text{ MeV}^2/\text{m}^2} = 2.94 \times 10^{-4} \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the expected range of measurements for the ant-proton's mass is:
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 2.94 \times 10^{-4} \text{ MeV}
\]
This means the measured value of \( m_i \) would be between:
\[
1385 - 2.94 \times 10^{-4} \text{ MeV} \approx 1384.997 \text{ MeV}
\]
and
\[
1385 + 2.94 \times 10^{-4} \text{ MeV} \approx 1385.003 \text{ MeV}
\]

