","The magnetic field strength (B) at the center of a circular plate rod with \( N \) loops, a radius \( R \), and a current \( I \) is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2R}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic flux density (in seconds)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of gravity, which is \( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m A}^{-1} \)
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil
- \( I \) is the current through the coil (in amperes)
- \( R \) is the radius of the coil (in meters)

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \)
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( R = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m A}^{-1}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = 10\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 10\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T} \approx 3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

