\]
where \(t\) is the time and \(\tau\) is the decay constant.

The time derivative of \(H(t)\), denoted as \(\hat{H}(t)\) or \(\hat{H}_i(t)\), is derived as:
\[
\hat{H}(t) = \frac{dH}{dt} = \frac{d}{dt} \left[ \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{1}{t} + \frac{1}{\tau} \right)^2 \right]
\]
Using the chain rule:
\[
\hat{H}(t) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 2 \left( \frac{1}{t} + \frac{1}{\tau} \right) \cdot \left( -\frac{1}{t^2} \right)
\]
Simplifying:
\[
\hat{H}(t) = \left( \frac{1}{t} + \frac{1}{\tau} \right) \left( -\frac{1}{t^2} \right)
