What is the expression for the intensity of thermal radiation of a black body in terms of the temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The intensity of thermal radiation of a black tube as a function of temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \) is given by **Planck's formula**:

\[
B(\lambda, T) = \frac{2hc^2}{\lambda^5} \left( \frac{1}{e^{\frac{hc}{\lambda kT}} - 1} \right)
\]

where:

- \( B(\lambda, T) \) is the speed density of angular oscillations (energy per unit time, per unit area, per unit area, per unit distance in the wavelength axis).  
- \( h \) is Planck's constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \))
- \( c \) is the speed of light in vacuum (\( 3.00 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \))
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation
- \( T \) is the temperature in Kelvin
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( 1.381 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K} \))

This formula describes the spectrality of light emitted by a point above temperature \( T \).
